// Generated by tools/build_appellations.py from the Vitis Mundi atlas. Do not edit by hand.
APX.gr = {"country":{"name":"Greece","tagline":"Four thousand years of wine on islands and mountains, now poured as some of the most electric whites and age-worthy reds of the Mediterranean.","classification":"Greek wine labels follow the EU pyramid. At the top sit the 33 PDOs (ΠΟΠ, Prostatevomeni Onomasia Proelefsis), most registered in 1981 when Greece joined the European Community; their rules fix varieties, yields and styles, and several are tied to a single grape (Naoussa to Xinomavro, Nemea to Agiorgitiko, Mantinia to Moschofilero, Patra to Roditis). Below them more than a hundred PGIs (ΠΓΕ) range from regional names such as Macedonia or Peloponnese to small zones such as Florina, Drama or Plagies Ampelou on Samos, and allow international varieties and blends. Varietal wines without geographical indication and plain wine sit at the bottom.\n\nThree things trip up newcomers. First, many of the best bottles are PGIs, because estates blend native and international grapes or work outside a PDO's grape rules. Second, Greece keeps traditional terms: Retsina is protected as a 'traditional appellation' for resinated wine, Verdea for the oxidative whites of Zakynthos, and Vinsanto belongs to Santorini alone. Third, ageing words differ by category: Reserve and Grand Reserve are reserved for PDO wines, while Cava is used for aged PGI and table wines. Ktima means estate, Monastiri a monastery, Archontiko a manor."},"regions":[{"name":"Macedonia","slug":"macedonia","tagline":"Xinomavro country: cool mountain slopes, lakes and Greece's most age-worthy reds.","ids":["gr-amyntaio","gr-goumenissa","gr-naoussa","gr-plagies-melitona"]},{"name":"Thessaly","slug":"thessaly","tagline":"Olympus foothills, the Pagasetic Gulf and the revival of Limniona.","ids":["gr-anchialos","gr-mesenikola","gr-rapsani"]},{"name":"Epirus","slug":"epirus","tagline":"Wet, mountainous north-west Greece and the sparkling Debina of Zitsa.","ids":["gr-zitsa"]},{"name":"Peloponnese","slug":"peloponnese","tagline":"Greece's biggest wine region: Nemea's reds, Mantinia's aromatic whites and the sweet wines of Patras.","ids":["gr-mantinia","gr-mavrodafni-patron","gr-monemvasia-malvasia","gr-moschato-patron","gr-moschatos-riou-patras","gr-nemea","gr-patra"]},{"name":"Ionian Islands","slug":"ionian-islands","tagline":"Venetian islands and the limestone Robola of Kefalonia.","ids":["gr-mavrodafni-kefallinias","gr-moschatos-kefallinias","gr-robola-kefallinias"]},{"name":"Aegean Islands","slug":"aegean-islands","tagline":"Muscat islands: Samos, Limnos and the ancient amphora ports of Chios and Thasos.","ids":["gr-limnos","gr-moschatos-limnou","gr-samos"]},{"name":"Cyclades","slug":"cyclades","tagline":"Wind, pumice and Assyrtiko: Santorini and Paros.","ids":["gr-malvasia-paros","gr-paros","gr-santorini"]},{"name":"Dodecanese","slug":"dodecanese","tagline":"Rhodes: Athiri on Mount Attavyros and Italian-era sparkling.","ids":["gr-moschatos-rodou","gr-rodos"]},{"name":"Crete","slug":"crete","tagline":"Minoan wine presses, Vidiano and the Liatiko of Dafnes and Sitia.","ids":["gr-archanes","gr-chandakas-candia","gr-dafnes","gr-malvasia-chandakas-candia","gr-malvasia-sitias","gr-peza","gr-sitia"]}],"items":{"gr-amyntaio":{"name":"Amyndeon","cat":"PDO","region":"Macedonia","created":"1971 (EU register 1981)","styles":["red","rose","sparkling"],"grapes":"Xinomavro","summary":"The coolest PDO in Greece sits on a high plateau in western Macedonia at 570 to 750 metres, around the lakes Vegoritida, Petron, Zazari and Chimaditida. That altitude and the lakes' moderating effect make Amyndeon the natural home of sparkling and rosé Xinomavro as well as fresher, more aromatic reds than Naoussa's. It is also one of the few PDOs in Greece that allows rosé and sparkling wine.","terroir":"Sandy soils in parts of the zone resisted phylloxera, so some ungrafted old Xinomavro survives. Frost and a short season are the risks.","producers":["Alpha Estate","Kir-Yianni (Samaropetra)","Karanika","Ktima Vegoritis"],"nerd_note":"Alpha Estate's ungrafted Xinomavro block 'Hedgehog' sits on sandy soil near the lake.","rslug":"macedonia"},"gr-anchialos":{"name":"Anchialos","cat":"PDO","region":"Thessaly","created":"1982","styles":["white"],"grapes":"Roditis and Savatiano","summary":"A small white-wine PDO on the shore of the Pagasetic Gulf near Volos, blending Roditis and Savatiano into fresh, lemony wines for local fish tavernas. Most production is cooperative. The wines are light, crisp and meant for drinking young, with Roditis giving citrus freshness and Savatiano a little more body.","terroir":"Flat to gently sloping coastal land with sea breezes; light, calcareous soils.","rslug":"thessaly"},"gr-archanes":{"name":"Archanes","cat":"PDO","region":"Crete","created":"1971 (EU register 1981)","styles":["red"],"grapes":"Kotsifali and Mandilari","summary":"A red-only PDO around Archanes, a Minoan centre where the villa of Vathypetro preserves one of the oldest wine presses in Europe. Kotsifali gives perfume and alcohol, Mandilari colour and grip.","terroir":"Slopes beneath Mount Giouchtas at 300 to 600 metres.","producers":["Lyrarakis","Archanes Cooperative","Digenakis"],"nerd_note":"Vathypetro's Minoan press, a few kilometres south of Archanes, is still visible in situ.","rslug":"crete"},"gr-chandakas-candia":{"name":"Chandakas-Candia","cat":"PDO","region":"Crete","created":"2011","styles":["white","red"],"grapes":"Vidiano, Vilana, Assyrtiko, Athiri, Thrapsathiri (white); Kotsifali, Mandilari (red)","summary":"Created in 2011 around Heraklion (Chandakas was its Byzantine and Arab name, Candia its Venetian one), this PDO gives a home to modern Cretan dry wines, including Vidiano-based whites. White blends lean on Vidiano, Vilana, Thrapsathiri, Assyrtiko and Athiri; reds on Kotsifali and Mandilari.","rslug":"crete"},"gr-dafnes":{"name":"Dafnes","cat":"PDO","region":"Crete","created":"1971 (EU register 1981)","styles":["red","sweet"],"grapes":"Liatiko (with Mandilari permitted)","summary":"West of Heraklion, Dafnes is the home of Liatiko, an early-ripening Cretan red that gives pale, spicy dry wines and, traditionally, sweet wines from sun-dried grapes. Douloufakis is the leading estate.","terroir":"Hills of 300 to 600 metres, calcareous clay.","producers":["Douloufakis","Silva Daskalaki"],"rslug":"crete"},"gr-goumenissa":{"name":"Goumenissa","cat":"PDO","region":"Macedonia","created":"1979 (EU register 1981)","styles":["red"],"grapes":"Xinomavro with at least 20% Negoska","summary":"On the lower slopes of Mount Paiko north-west of Thessaloniki, Goumenissa blends Xinomavro with the softer, deeply coloured Negoska. The result is rounder and earlier-drinking than Naoussa but keeps Xinomavro's spice and acidity. Small family estates dominate.","terroir":"Paiko's foothills at 200 to 400 metres, sandy clay and limestone, a more open, slightly warmer position than Naoussa.","producers":["Tatsis","Aidarinis","Chatzivariti","Boutari (Filiria)"],"nerd_note":"Negoska is grown almost nowhere else; its name probably comes from the old Slavic name of Naoussa.","rslug":"macedonia"},"gr-limnos":{"name":"Limnos","cat":"PDO","region":"Aegean Islands","created":"1971 (EU register 1981)","styles":["white","sweet","red"],"grapes":"Muscat of Alexandria (white); Limnio (red)","summary":"Limnos makes dry, floral white wine from Muscat of Alexandria, unusual in Greece, and reds from Limnio, a grape probably identical to the Lemnia vine that Aristotle described. Volcanic soils and constant wind keep yields low.","terroir":"Volcanic and clay soils on a flat, windy island in the north Aegean.","producers":["Chatzigeorgiou","Limnos Cooperative"],"rslug":"aegean-islands"},"gr-malvasia-chandakas-candia":{"name":"Malvasia Chandakas-Candia","cat":"PDO","region":"Crete","created":"2011","styles":["sweet"],"grapes":"Malvasia di Candia Aromatica, Vidiano, Muscat Blanc, Liatiko, Thrapsathiri, Assyrtiko, Athiri","summary":"The Heraklion version of the Malvasia revival: sweet wines from sun-dried grapes, reviving the style Venetian Candia exported across Europe. Malvasia di Candia Aromatica leads the blend with Vidiano, Muscat Blanc, Liatiko, Thrapsathiri, Assyrtiko and Athiri permitted. Production is small; Heraklion-area producers are still building the style.","rslug":"crete"},"gr-malvasia-paros":{"name":"Malvasia Paros","cat":"PDO","region":"Cyclades","created":"2011","styles":["sweet"],"grapes":"Monemvasia with up to 15% Assyrtiko","summary":"One of the four Malvasia PDOs created in 2011: sweet wine from sun-dried Monemvasia grapes, aged in oak. Production is tiny. Its rules allow Monemvasia with a little Assyrtiko. Like the other three Malvasia PDOs of 2011, it aims to revive the sweet wine style Venetian merchants once traded from the Aegean.","rslug":"cyclades"},"gr-malvasia-sitias":{"name":"Malvasia Sitia","cat":"PDO","region":"Crete","created":"2011","styles":["sweet"],"grapes":"Malvasia di Candia Aromatica, Muscat Blanc, Liatiko, Thrapsathiri, Assyrtiko, Athiri","summary":"A 2011 PDO for sweet wines in the medieval Cretan Malvasia tradition, made from sun-dried grapes around Sitia. Malvasia di Candia Aromatica, Muscat Blanc, Liatiko, Thrapsathiri, Assyrtiko and Athiri are permitted. Production is still tiny, but it gives eastern Crete a sweet-wine identity alongside dry Sitia.","rslug":"crete"},"gr-mantinia":{"name":"Mantinia","cat":"PDO","region":"Peloponnese","created":"1971 (EU register 1981)","styles":["white","sparkling"],"grapes":"Moschofilero (at least 85%) with Asproudes","summary":"On a high plateau around Tripoli in Arcadia, at roughly 650 metres, Mantinia makes the most aromatic dry white in Greece from pink-skinned Moschofilero: rose petal, citrus blossom and spice, with crisp acidity and moderate alcohol. Sparkling Mantinia is permitted, and the cool, late harvest (often into October) makes this one of the latest-picked PDOs in Greece.","terroir":"Cold winters, frost risk and a short season on the plateau of ancient Mantineia; clay and limestone soils.","producers":["Tselepos","Troupis","Bosinakis","Spiropoulos","Boutari"],"nerd_note":"Moschofilero is a 'grey' grape: skin colour ranges from pale pink to purple, and longer skin contact gives copper-tinted whites and rosés.","rslug":"peloponnese"},"gr-mavrodafni-kefallinias":{"name":"Mavrodaphne of Kefalonia","cat":"PDO","region":"Ionian Islands","created":"1971 (EU register 1981)","styles":["fortified","sweet","red"],"grapes":"Mavrodaphne with Korinthiaki","summary":"Kefalonia's fortified red, made in the same style as Mavrodaphne of Patras. Mavrodaphne may in fact originate in the Ionian islands, and Kefalonian producers also make it dry. EU rules allow Korinthiaki (Black Corinth) alongside Mavrodaphne, as in Patras.","rslug":"ionian-islands"},"gr-mavrodafni-patron":{"name":"Mavrodaphne of Patras","cat":"PDO","region":"Peloponnese","created":"1971 (EU register 1981)","styles":["fortified","sweet","red"],"grapes":"Mavrodaphne (at least 51%) with Korinthiaki (Black Corinth)","summary":"A fortified red made since the nineteenth century around Patras, sweet and raisiny with notes of coffee, caramel and dried fig, often aged oxidatively for years in barrels. Achaia Clauss made it famous; Parparoussis and Antonopoulos have revived higher-quality versions, including old solera-style bottlings.","terroir":"Coastal hills around Patras.","producers":["Achaia Clauss","Parparoussis","Cavino"],"nerd_note":"Local lore says Gustav Clauss named the wine after his late fiancée Daphne; the grape name 'black laurel' is older than that story.","rslug":"peloponnese"},"gr-mesenikola":{"name":"Mesenikola","cat":"PDO","region":"Thessaly","created":"1996","styles":["red"],"grapes":"Mavro Messenikola with Carignan and Syrah","summary":"Named after a village near Lake Plastiras in the foothills of the Agrafa mountains, this PDO is built around the rare local Mavro Messenikola, softened with Carignan and Syrah. The wines are light, peppery reds; very little leaves the region.","terroir":"Hillside vineyards above the Thessalian plain at around 200 to 500 metres.","rslug":"thessaly"},"gr-monemvasia-malvasia":{"name":"Monemvasia-Malvasia","cat":"PDO","region":"Peloponnese","created":"2011","styles":["sweet"],"grapes":"Monemvasia (at least 51%), Asproudes, Kydonitsa, Assyrtiko","summary":"Registered in 2011 to revive the legendary Malmsey of the Middle Ages, this PDO covers sweet wines from sun-dried grapes made in Laconia around the rock of Monemvasia. The rules require long barrel ageing, and the best wines are amber, honeyed and nutty. Monemvasia Winery (Tsimbidis) led the revival.","terroir":"Hills of south-eastern Laconia with limestone soils, hot and dry.","producers":["Monemvasia Winery (Tsimbidis)"],"nerd_note":"The grape called Monemvasia and the Italian Malvasia family share a name and a trade route, but DNA shows Monemvasia is a distinct Greek variety.","rslug":"peloponnese"},"gr-moschato-patron":{"name":"Muscat of Patras","cat":"PDO","region":"Peloponnese","created":"1971 (EU register 1981)","styles":["sweet","fortified"],"grapes":"Muscat Blanc à Petits Grains","summary":"Sweet Muscat from the hills around Patras, either fortified or made from sun-dried grapes; fragrant with orange blossom and apricot. The grape is the small-berried Muscat Blanc (Moschato Aspro), the same as on Samos, grown on the hills behind the city alongside the vineyards of PDO Patra and Mavrodaphne of Patras.","terroir":"Slopes of Achaia with calcareous soils.","rslug":"peloponnese"},"gr-moschatos-kefallinias":{"name":"Muscat of Kefalonia","cat":"PDO","region":"Ionian Islands","created":"1971 (EU register 1981)","styles":["sweet","fortified"],"grapes":"Muscat Blanc à Petits Grains","summary":"Sweet Muscat from Kefalonia, fortified or from sun-dried grapes; small production, mostly sold on the island. The grape is Muscat Blanc à Petits Grains, grown on the same dry limestone as Robola. Like Mavrodaphne of Kefalonia, it is a small production sold mostly on the island.","rslug":"ionian-islands"},"gr-moschatos-limnou":{"name":"Muscat of Limnos","cat":"PDO","region":"Aegean Islands","created":"1971 (EU register 1981)","styles":["sweet","fortified"],"grapes":"Muscat of Alexandria","summary":"The sweet partner to PDO Limnos, fortified or from sun-dried grapes; grapey and floral. Unlike the Muscat Blanc of Samos and Patras, it is made from Muscat of Alexandria (Moschato Alexandreias), fortified or from sun-dried grapes, on the island's volcanic soils.","rslug":"aegean-islands"},"gr-moschatos-riou-patras":{"name":"Muscat of Rio Patras","cat":"PDO","region":"Peloponnese","created":"1971 (EU register 1981)","styles":["sweet","fortified"],"grapes":"Muscat Blanc à Petits Grains","summary":"A smaller Muscat appellation centred on Rio, east of Patras at the narrows of the Gulf of Corinth, for fortified or sun-dried sweet Muscat. Parparoussis makes the reference examples.","terroir":"Coastal hills around Rio.","producers":["Parparoussis"],"rslug":"peloponnese"},"gr-moschatos-rodou":{"name":"Muscat of Rhodes","cat":"PDO","region":"Dodecanese","created":"1971 (EU register 1981)","styles":["sweet"],"grapes":"Muscat Blanc à Petits Grains, Muscat Trani","summary":"Sweet Muscat from Rhodes, light and floral; a small appellation, mostly cooperative. The vineyards share the slopes of the island with PDO Rhodes, and the wine is usually lighter and more floral than the Muscats of Samos or Patras. Production is small.","rslug":"dodecanese"},"gr-naoussa":{"name":"Naoussa","cat":"PDO","region":"Macedonia","created":"1971 (EU register 1981)","styles":["red"],"grapes":"Xinomavro 100%","summary":"Naoussa is the home of Xinomavro and Greece's most serious red wine appellation. The town sits on the eastern flank of Mount Vermio, and its vineyards run along the slopes at roughly 150 to 450 metres, with Kir-Yianni, Thymiopoulos, Dalamara, Karydas, Chrisohoou and the founding house of Boutari (1879) at the core of the local tradition. The wine must be pure Xinomavro, a grape whose name means 'acid-black' though it is rarely black: in the glass Naoussa is translucent garnet, with high acidity, firm tannins and a perfume of sour cherry, tomato leaf, olive, dried rose and spice that develops towards leather and forest floor over ten to twenty years.\n\nThe comparison with Barolo is inevitable and partly fair. Like Nebbiolo, Xinomavro is sensitive to site and vintage, slow to reveal itself and unforgiving of over-extraction. The modern debate in Naoussa mirrors Piedmont's: large old casks versus new oak, early drinking versus long ageing, single-vineyard bottlings versus blends of villages. Growers increasingly name their sites, from Trilofo and Fytia in the south to Gastra, Yianakochori and Ramnista.","terroir":"Mount Vermio shelters the vineyards from cold north-westerly winds and drains cool air down the slopes at night. Soils vary from marl and clay to limestone and sandy loam; sandier sites give lighter, more perfumed wines, clay sites darker, firmer ones. The continental climate gives cold winters and warm summers with a long autumn.","producers":["Boutari","Kir-Yianni","Thymiopoulos","Dalamara","Karydas","Chrisohoou","Foundi","Diamantakos","Markovitis"],"nerd_note":"Kir-Yianni was founded in 1997 by Yiannis Boutaris, who left his family firm to make site-driven Xinomavro; he later became mayor of Thessaloniki.","rslug":"macedonia"},"gr-nemea":{"name":"Nemea","cat":"PDO","region":"Peloponnese","created":"1971 (EU register 1981)","area_ha":3000,"styles":["red","sweet","fortified"],"grapes":"Agiorgitiko 100%","summary":"Nemea is Greece's largest red wine appellation and the home of Agiorgitiko (St George's grape). It covers 17 villages in the hills of Korinthia, plus two (Gymno and Malandreni) in north-western Argolida, making it the only Greek PDO spread across two prefectures. Agiorgitiko is the Merlot of Greece in its generosity: soft tannins, plum, cherry and sweet spice, but with enough freshness at altitude to age well. Styles range from fresh, fruity reds to oak-aged reserves and rarer semi-sweet, fortified and sun-dried sweet wines; rosé and blends are made but must be labelled as PGI.\n\nThe appellation links itself to the ancient Phliasian wine and to the myth of Heracles and the Nemean lion; locals still call the wine 'blood of Hercules'. The Temple of Zeus at Ancient Nemea stands among the vines.","terroir":"Vineyards climb from about 230 metres on the valley floor to around 850 metres. Producers talk of three altitude bands: a low zone (Nemea, Ancient Nemea, Ancient Kleones, Leontio, Gymno) of richer red clay; a middle zone (Koutsi, Dafni) widely considered the sweet spot for structured reds; and a high zone (Asprokampos, Psari, Kastraki, Kefalari and others) of cooler, stonier sites that give fresher wines. These sub-zones are informal and not codified in the PDO rules; proposals to formalise them have been discussed for years.","producers":["Gaia Wines","Domaine Skouras","Palivou","Papaioannou","Semeli","Lafazanis","Harlaftis","Mitravelas","Driopi (Tselepos)"],"nerd_note":"Koutsi has the reputation of a grand cru, and wines from it often carry the village name in fantasy brand names even though the PDO does not allow sub-zone labelling.","rslug":"peloponnese"},"gr-paros":{"name":"Paros","cat":"PDO","region":"Cyclades","created":"1982","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Mandilaria with the white Monemvasia (red); Monemvasia (white)","summary":"Paros has one of the oddest rules in Europe: its red must blend the dark Mandilaria with the white Monemvasia, a historic practice that softens Mandilaria's tannin and colour. Moraitis is the main producer. Old bush vines on sand and schist are often ungrafted.","terroir":"Sandy, granite and schist soils, strong winds.","producers":["Moraitis","Paros Winery"],"rslug":"cyclades"},"gr-patra":{"name":"Patra","cat":"PDO","region":"Peloponnese","created":"1971 (EU register 1981)","styles":["white"],"grapes":"Roditis 100%","summary":"The dry white appellation of Patras, made only from Roditis grown on the slopes of the Achaean mountains. Higher vineyards give citrus, minerally wines; many producers bottle Roditis as PGI instead.","terroir":"Slopes from about 250 to 800 metres facing the Gulf of Patras.","rslug":"peloponnese"},"gr-peza":{"name":"Peza","cat":"PDO","region":"Crete","created":"1971 (EU register 1981)","styles":["white","red"],"grapes":"Vilana (white); Kotsifali and Mandilari (red)","summary":"South of Heraklion around the village of Peza, Crete's largest PDO makes fresh whites from Vilana and reds from Kotsifali blended with Mandilari for colour and tannin. The Peza Union of cooperatives remains important, alongside family estates such as Lyrarakis and Stilianou.","terroir":"Hillsides at roughly 300 to 600 metres under the Lasithi and Psiloritis ranges; calcareous clay.","producers":["Peza Union","Lyrarakis","Stilianou","Minos Wines"],"rslug":"crete"},"gr-plagies-melitona":{"name":"Slopes of Meliton","cat":"PDO","region":"Macedonia","created":"1982","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Red: Limnio with Cabernet Sauvignon and Cabernet Franc; white: Athiri, Roditis, Assyrtiko","summary":"A one-estate appellation on the Sithonia peninsula of Halkidiki, created for Domaine Porto Carras, which John Carras planted from the 1960s with advice from Émile Peynaud. Its red blend of Limnio and Cabernet was the first Greek appellation to include international varieties, and the estate trained a generation of Greek winemakers, including Evangelos Gerovassiliou.","terroir":"Granite and schist slopes above the sea on Mount Meliton (Itamos).","producers":["Domaine Porto Carras"],"nerd_note":"Malagousia's rescue began here: Professor Vassilis Logothetis collected the near-extinct vine and Gerovassiliou propagated it at Porto Carras before planting it at Epanomi.","rslug":"macedonia"},"gr-rapsani":{"name":"Rapsani","cat":"PDO","region":"Thessaly","created":"1971 (EU register 1981)","styles":["red"],"grapes":"Xinomavro, Krassato and Stavroto","summary":"Rapsani is the Mount Olympus appellation, a historic field blend of Xinomavro with two local grapes, Krassato and Stavroto, grown on the mountain's south-eastern slopes above the Tempi valley. Tsantali revived it from 1991 and still dominates; the wines are spicy, medium-bodied and grow more refined as the vineyards climb.","terroir":"Schist soils between about 300 and 700 metres on Olympus, with the Aegean a few kilometres east. Producers distinguish lower, middle and upper zones.","producers":["Tsantali","Ktima Liappis","Dougos"],"nerd_note":"Tsantali's Rapsani Grande Reserve comes from the highest, schist-rich parcels, which locals call the 'old vines of the gods'.","rslug":"thessaly"},"gr-robola-kefallinias":{"name":"Robola of Kefalonia","cat":"PDO","region":"Ionian Islands","created":"1971 (EU register 1981)","styles":["white"],"grapes":"Robola 100%","summary":"Kefalonia's flagship white grows on the limestone flanks of Mount Ainos, often around the Omala valley, giving lean, lemony, stony wines with a smoky edge. Many vines are ungrafted because phylloxera could not spread in the loose limestone scree. The Robola cooperative (Orealios Gaia) handles much of the crop.","terroir":"Limestone scree at up to around 900 metres, very poor soils, strong sun and sea winds.","producers":["Gentilini","Sclavos","Orealios Gaia (Robola Cooperative)","Haritatos"],"nerd_note":"Robola was long assumed to be Italy's Ribolla Gialla; DNA analysis says the two are unrelated.","rslug":"ionian-islands"},"gr-rodos":{"name":"Rhodes","cat":"PDO","region":"Dodecanese","created":"1971 (EU register 1981)","styles":["white","red","rose","sparkling"],"grapes":"Athiri (white and sparkling); Mandilaria (red)","summary":"Rhodes' PDO covers delicate whites and sparkling wine from Athiri grown on the slopes of Mount Attavyros around Embonas, and reds from Mandilaria (locally Amorgiano). CAIR, founded under Italian rule in 1928, has made sparkling wine here for nearly a century.","terroir":"Limestone slopes up to about 700 metres on the island's highest mountain.","producers":["CAIR","Emery","Kounakis"],"rslug":"dodecanese"},"gr-samos":{"name":"Samos","cat":"PDO","region":"Aegean Islands","created":"1971 (EU register 1981)","styles":["sweet","fortified"],"grapes":"Muscat Blanc à Petits Grains","summary":"Muscat of Samos has been one of Greece's most exported wines for over a century, and it is a model of cooperative winemaking: the Union of Vinicultural Cooperatives of Samos, founded in 1934, buys grapes from thousands of growers who tend small terraces on the island's mountains. The range runs from fortified vin doux (Samos Doux, Grand Cru) to Nectar, made from sun-dried grapes and aged in oak, and long-aged Anthemis and Samos Vin Doux Naturel Grand Cru bottlings.\n\nAltitude matters: terraces climb from near sea level to over 800 metres on Mount Ampelos and Mount Kerkis, and the higher sites ripen weeks later, giving finer, more aromatic Muscat. The same mountains are covered by PGI Plagies Ampelou, used for dry wines.","terroir":"Steep, hand-built stone terraces of schist and limestone; the north-facing slopes are cooler and wetter.","producers":["Union of Vinicultural Cooperatives of Samos (EOSS)","Vakakis"],"nerd_note":"Samos was a tax-free supplier of Muscat to the Vatican and the Catholic church for sacramental wine in the twentieth century.","rslug":"aegean-islands"},"gr-santorini":{"name":"Santorini","cat":"PDO","region":"Cyclades","created":"1971 (EU register 1981)","area_ha":1200,"styles":["white","sweet"],"grapes":"Assyrtiko (at least 75% for dry white), Athiri, Aidani; Vinsanto mostly Assyrtiko with Aidani","summary":"Santorini makes the most distinctive white wine in Greece and one of the most distinctive anywhere. The island is the rim of a collapsed volcano: the Bronze Age eruption that buried Akrotiri left a blanket of ash and pumice with almost no organic matter and no clay, and on it grows Assyrtiko, a grape that keeps searing acidity under a hot sun. Dry Santorini (at least 75% Assyrtiko, the rest Athiri and Aidani) tastes of lemon, flint and sea spray, gains weight and smoky complexity with age, and is the reason Assyrtiko is now planted from Macedonia to South Australia.\n\nTwo traditional styles carry the island's history. Nykteri, from 'night work', was originally picked and pressed at night to avoid the heat; today it is a richer, oak-aged dry white. Vinsanto, the island's sweet wine (the name probably means 'wine of Santorini', not 'holy wine'), is made from grapes dried in the sun for around two weeks and aged at least two years in oak, giving a dark, figgy, acid-lit dessert wine that can outlive its makers.\n\nScarcity is part of the story. The island has about 1,200 hectares of vines, shrinking under hotels and villas, and yields are tiny: drought, wind and old vines often give a fraction of the legal limit, and the 2023 and 2024 crops were painfully small. Prices for top bottlings have risen sharply.","terroir":"No clay means no phylloxera, so vines grow on their own roots, some root systems claimed to be centuries old; growers periodically cut the old trunk away and let a new shoot grow from the same roots. The vines are trained low as kouloures, baskets woven from the canes, with the grapes hanging inside, out of the wind and sun. Night humidity from the sea is absorbed by the porous pumice and keeps unirrigated vines alive through summers with almost no rain. Sub-zones such as Pyrgos, Megalochori, Akrotiri, Oia and Imerovigli give perceptibly different wines, and producers now bottle them separately.","producers":["Domaine Sigalas","Hatzidakis","Gaia Wines","Estate Argyros","Santo Wines","Vassaltis","Karamolegos","Gavalas","Venetsanos","Boutari"],"nerd_note":"The basket vines are rewoven every winter and can be 'rejuvenated' by cutting back to the root, so a vine's above-ground part may be young while its roots are hundreds of years old.","rslug":"cyclades"},"gr-sitia":{"name":"Sitia","cat":"PDO","region":"Crete","created":"1971 (EU register 1981)","styles":["red","white","sweet"],"grapes":"Liatiko and Mandilari (red); Vilana and Thrapsathiri (white)","summary":"Eastern Crete's PDO, with some of the island's highest vineyards (up to around 600 metres or more), makes Liatiko reds, white blends and sweet Liatiko. Economou's long-aged Liatiko from the Ziros plateau is a cult bottle.","terroir":"Dry, windy eastern Crete; terraces and plateaus above Sitia.","producers":["Economou","Sitia Cooperative","Yiannis Economou"],"rslug":"crete"},"gr-zitsa":{"name":"Zitsa","cat":"PDO","region":"Epirus","created":"1971 (EU register 1981)","styles":["white","sparkling"],"grapes":"Debina","summary":"In the mountains north of Ioannina, Zitsa makes still and lightly sparkling white wine from Debina, a high-acid grape with apple and white-flower aromas. The semi-sparkling style is traditional, and the region was praised by Byron, who stayed at the monastery of Profitis Ilias in 1809.","terroir":"Around 600 metres, high rainfall, cool nights, clay-limestone soils. Grapes ripen late and keep low alcohol.","producers":["Glinavos","Zoinos"],"nerd_note":"Debina is one of very few Greek grapes routinely made as a pétillant wine in a traditional regional style.","rslug":"epirus"}}};
